  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1239 PM MST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nLooking at Water Vapor, the upper level shortwave is moving\nthrough the area to the east, taking the strongest upper level \nwinds with it. Winds will gradually decrease into the evening, \ndropping off significantly a little after sunset. We'll see very \nbrief upper level ridging in between our two systems on Thursday. \nAn upper level trough will move onto the northwest coast of the\nU.S. on Thursday, approaching the area on Friday. A little \nshortwave will precede the main trough late Thursday into early \nFriday, bringing snow chances to the mountains during this time. \nPacific moisture will move into the higher terrain with this \nsystem at low to mid levels, helping with precip chances in the \nmountains. Moisture will generally remain higher to the west of \nour area, with the best chances of snow for our CWA in the Park \nRange.\n\nThe main trough will approach the region late Friday, moving through \non Saturday. Surface low pressure will develop on the lee side of \nthe mountains ahead of this system. This low will drag a cold front \nthrough the area late Friday into early Saturday, bringing much \ncooler temperatures.\n\nWe could briefly sit in the right entrance of the upper level jet \nSaturday morning. At the same time we'll see broad upper level \nsupport ahead of the trough. Unfortunately, moisture throughout \nthe column doesn't look great, but there should be enough for snow\nshowers in the higher terrain and potentially some light showers \nin the plains as well. Behind the overnight front, a few models \nare showing weak upslope flow across the southern foothills and \nPalmer Divide area in the mid to late morning and early afternoon.\nThis could be enough to trigger some shower activity in the \nsouthern foothills and Palmer Divide area, potentially extending \ninto the southern metro. Right now, the NBM has a medium chance \n(30% to 60%) that the southern metro, Palmer, and southern \nfoothills get at least a trace of snow, with very low chances \n(<10%) that Denver sees more than an inch. For the higher terrain,\nsnow levels will start out around 6000ft, dropping closer to \n4000ft by Saturday morning. As mentioned previously, moisture with\nthis event isn't particularly high, so overall accumulation's \nwill be on the lower end, with liquid amounts generally around a \nfew hundredths to a quarter of an inch (with potential for higher \namounts in the Park Range). \n\nBehind the front, conditions are expected to be much cooler. Lows \nSaturday morning in the plains will drop into the 20s to mid 30s.\nLows for Sunday into the middle of next week are forecast to be \nin the teens to mid 20s for the plains with highs only reaching \ninto the 40s each afternoon. Another shortwave trough is forecast \nto approach the area late Monday into early Tuesday, bringing\nadditional snow chances to the mountains.",
